+### JSON Patch diff — `createPatch(from, to)`
+
+Produces the [JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902) that turns one version of a
+document into another, so that a client that has both can send what changed instead of the whole new
+version.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch, createPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const from = { name: 'config', tags: ['a', 'b'], retries: 1 };
+const to = { name: 'config', tags: ['a', 'b'], retries: 3 };
+
+createPatch(from, to); // [{ op: 'replace', path: '/retries', value: 3 }]
+applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to)); // { name: 'config', tags: ['a', 'b'], retries: 3 }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` is `to`, for any two JSON documents. That is the one thing
+the patch always does; everything below is about what it does *not* contain.
+
+**It names only the locations the two versions disagree about.** Objects are compared member by
+member and arrays element by element, as deep as the two documents go, and a subtree they share stops
+the comparison where it starts — so no operation ever carries a value both versions already have.
+Two documents that are equal as JSON give an empty patch, and two that differ in one member deep
+inside give one operation naming it:
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ a: { b: { c: 1 }, d: 2 } }, { a: { b: { c: 9 }, d: 2 } });
+// [{ op: 'replace', path: '/a/b/c', value: 9 }]
+```
+
+**Arrays are compared as sequences, not position by position.** An element both versions hold is kept
+wherever it has moved to, so an insertion at the front of a thousand elements is one `add` and not a
+thousand `replace`s, and a change inside one element is an operation inside that element:
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ items: ['a', 'b'] }, { items: ['z', 'a', 'b'] });
+// [{ op: 'add', path: '/items/0', value: 'z' }]
+
+createPatch([{ id: 'a', n: 1 }, { id: 'b', n: 2 }], [{ id: 'b', n: 2 }]);
+// [{ op: 'remove', path: '/0' }]
+```
+
+An element added at the end of an array is added at `-`, the position after the last one. The indices
+of the other operations are those of the array *as the patch is applied*, which the operations
+themselves shift: two `remove`s of neighbouring elements name the same index twice.
+
+**`add`, `remove` and `replace`, and no other operation.** A value that moved is described as a
+`remove` and an `add` rather than a `move`, and a value that appears twice as two `add`s rather than a
+`copy`: both would have to be hunted for across the document, and neither makes the patch say
+anything the three plain operations do not. There is no `test` either — the patch states what changed,
+not what the document must look like first. A caller who wants a precondition prepends their own
+`test`, which is the conditional update RFC 6902, section 5 describes.
+
+The patch is not the shortest one that could do the job: a document that lost a hundred members gives
+a hundred `remove`s, where a single `replace` of their parent would have been one operation. That is
+the trade this entry point makes — it never sends a value the two versions share, and a `replace` of
+the parent would send all the members that stayed.
+
+Taking a diff cannot fail, and there is no error to catch: any two JSON documents differ in a way
+these three operations express, so — as with `applyMergePatch` — no class is published for a failure
+that does not exist. Values outside the JSON data model are refused as everywhere else, by the same
+`TypeError`, which says whether it was `from` or `to` that held one.
+
+Both documents are left alone, and the patch shares nothing with either: its values are copies, so
+changing the patch afterwards cannot change the document it came from, and changing that document
+cannot change the patch. The same two documents always give the same patch, whatever order their
+members were written in.
+
+Comparing two arrays costs a table of one cell per pair of elements between their common ends, so two
+long arrays that differ in the middle are the expensive case; an append, a single insertion or one
+changed element costs a walk.
+
