+
+# createPatch describes only what changed, which is not the shortest patch
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` has to return a patch that turns one document into the other, and that
+requirement alone is met by one operation: `{ "op": "replace", "path": "", "value": to }`. The reason
+the entry point exists is the rest of the request — a client that has both versions should send what
+changed, not the whole new version — so the interesting question is not whether the patch applies but
+what it is allowed to contain. That has to be settled before the algorithm, because two sensible
+objectives pull in opposite directions:
+
+- **the fewest operations** — the patch is shortest when a location whose contents changed a lot is
+  replaced whole, so `{ "a": { "x": 1, "y": 2, "z": 3 } }` becoming `{ "a": { "x": 9, "y": 8, "z": 3 } }`
+  is one `replace` of `/a` rather than two of `/a/x` and `/a/y`;
+- **the least data** — the patch carries nothing the two versions already agree on, so the same change
+  is two operations, and `z` is not sent again.
+
+Minimising operations is what several diff implementations do, and it is the wrong objective here. It
+licenses exactly what the request rules out: a document that differs in one small place but whose
+parent is large enough gets sent again under the name of a `replace`. The whole-document `replace`
+above is only the extreme of that, and no rule that permits the small case forbids it in principle.
+
+## Decision
+
+The patch names only the locations the two versions disagree about, and carries no value they share.
+`createPatch` walks the two documents together, and at each location:
+
+- **values that are equal as JSON produce nothing.** Equality is RFC 6902, section 4.6's, the one
+  `test` uses, which is why it moved out of `json-patch.ts` into `json-value.ts` under dl-007 as a
+  shared internal rather than being read a second time. An unchanged subtree, however large, stops the
+  walk where it starts: this is the one test that keeps the patch to what changed.
+- **two objects are compared member by member.** A member only the first has is a `remove`, a member
+  only the second has is an `add` carrying that member's value, and a member both have is compared in
+  its turn. There is no size at which the comparison gives up and replaces the parent.
+- **two arrays are compared element by element**, which is dl-014.
+- **anything else is a `replace`** of the location with the new value: two values of different kinds,
+  or two scalars, have nothing inside them to tell apart. At the root that is a `replace` of `""`,
+  which RFC 6902 allows and dl-006 kept.
+
+**The patch is therefore not the shortest one that would work, and that is the trade.** A document
+that lost a hundred members gives a hundred `remove`s where one `replace` of their parent would have
+done, and two unrelated objects at one location give a `remove` per old member and an `add` per new
+one. In both cases the single `replace` would have to carry every member that stayed; the operations
+are more numerous, and the data on the wire is not larger. The README says so in those terms, so that
+a caller counting operations is not surprised.
+
+**Member names are visited in sorted order.** Operations on distinct members of one object do not
+depend on each other, so any order would apply, and the order `Object.keys` gives is the order the
+members happened to be written in — which would make the patch depend on how a document was built and
+not on what it holds. Sorting by code unit, with no locale in it, is what the determinism directive
+asks for and what dl-012 means by comparing documents as JSON and not as text: two documents that are
+equal as JSON give the same patch, character for character.
+
+**Nothing is `test`ed, `move`d or `copy`ed** — see dl-015 — and the patch is a fresh array of plain
+operation objects, so a caller may add to it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A patch for two versions that differ in one place is one operation naming that place, whatever the
+  size of the documents around it, which is the behaviour the request asked for and the README
+  promises.
+- A caller who wants the shortest patch rather than the smallest payload does not get it here. If a
+  service ever needs that — a transport where the operation count dominates — it is a second,
+  explicitly named entry point, as dl-004 answers the same shape of question about structural sharing.
+- The patch depends on the two documents alone, so it can be cached, logged and compared as a value.
+  The tests assert the exact patch for each case, which only holds because of the sorted order above.
+- The walk is proportional to the two documents, plus the cost of comparing the values it meets. Two
+  large documents that differ everywhere are compared twice over — once to find the difference, once
+  by the array alignment of dl-014 — which is the cost of a diff and not of a patch; the entry point
+  that applies a patch is unaffected.
