+status: approved
+---
+
+# Arrays are diffed as an edit script over their elements
+
+## Context
+
+dl-013 settled that `createPatch` carries no value the two versions share. For objects that is
+straightforward — a member is named, and the two versions either have it or not. Arrays are the hard
+case, and the one the request is really about, because an element is named by its *position*, and a
+single insertion moves every position after it.
+
+Three readings were available:
+
+- **replace the array whole** when it is not equal. One operation, and the whole new array on the
+  wire: this is "sending the whole document again" for the part of a document most likely to be long,
+  and dl-013 rules it out.
+- **compare position by position** — `/0` against `/0`, `/1` against `/1`, then add or remove the
+  tail. Cheap, and wrong in the common case: prepending one element to a thousand rewrites all
+  thousand, because every element is now compared with its neighbour. A log or a list of records
+  usually grows at one end and is read as a sequence, not as a mapping from index to value.
+- **compare as a sequence**, matching the elements the two versions have in common wherever they sit,
+  and naming only the elements added, removed and changed.
+
+RFC 6902 has nothing to say about any of this — it defines what a patch means, not how one is found —
+so this is the toolkit's choice, and it is the one place in this feature where there is a real
+algorithm rather than a reading of a specification.
+
+## Decision
+
+Two arrays are aligned as an edit script over their elements, and the operations follow the script.
+
+**An element the two versions share is kept wherever it has moved to.** The alignment counts a
+removal, an addition and a changed element as one step each, a shared element as none, and takes an
+alignment of the fewest steps. So one insertion at the front of a long array is one `add`, one
+element changed in place is an operation inside that element, and an element that moved is not
+resent.
+
+**The elements the two arrays begin and end with in common are matched first**, and only what is left
+between them is aligned. That is what makes the ordinary cases — an append, a single insertion, one
+changed element — a walk down the two arrays rather than a table, and it costs nothing in quality:
+there is always an alignment of the fewest steps that matches those elements.
+
+**What is left is aligned with a table of one cell per pair of elements**, holding the least number of
+steps that turns a prefix of one into a prefix of the other, and the script is read back out of it.
+Cells are 4 bytes (`Int32Array`), because this is where a diff of two long arrays spends its memory.
+Two long arrays that differ in the middle are therefore the expensive case, and the README says so.
+
+**An aligned pair that is not equal is compared in its turn, not replaced.** That is dl-013 applied
+one level down: two records that differ in one field give an operation naming that field. The
+alignment counts such a pair as one step even though it may produce several operations — it is an
+estimate made before the comparison it would have to run, and a cheap one; what it decides is which
+elements are paired, not what is sent for a pair.
+
+**Where two alignments cost the same, the choice is fixed**: an element equal on both sides is kept,
+then a removal, then an addition, and a changed element last. A tie is the normal case rather than the
+exception — a swap, a replaced element, a shifted list all offer several scripts of the same cost —
+so without a stated order the patch would depend on how the table happens to be read. With it, two
+documents always give the same patch, as dl-013 requires.
+
+**The indices in the patch are those of the array as the patch is applied, not of either version.**
+The operations shift the elements after them, so they are emitted in order while a cursor and a
+length are carried along: the cursor is where the element the next step is about has come to sit, a
+removal leaves it where it is because the tail has moved down, and an addition moves it past the
+element just inserted. Everything below the cursor is already what the second version has, and no
+later operation reaches below it, so an element the walk has passed is settled. Two `remove`s of
+neighbouring elements therefore name the same index twice, which reads like a bug and is not; the
+README says it, and a test asserts it.
+
+**An element added at the end of an array is added at `-`.** RFC 6902, section 4.1 gives `-` the
+meaning "the position after the last element", which is exactly what an append is; writing the length
+as an index would say the same thing in a form that looks like an off-by-one. Every other index is
+written out.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Appending to a long array, inserting into it, dropping an element from the front or changing one
+  field of one record are all one or two operations, independent of the length of the array. That is
+  the request's "one small place" for the shape of document where it matters most.
+- Comparing two arrays whose middles differ costs time and memory proportional to the product of
+  their lengths. A caller diffing two arrays of ten thousand mismatched elements will feel it; the
+  answer, as in dl-004 and dl-013, is a separate entry point with a different trade, not a cheaper
+  alignment here that would quietly resend data.
+- A reordering is described as removals and additions. The `move` operation would say it in one
+  operation, and dl-015 says why it is not used.
+- The patch's array indices only make sense applied in order, from the document the diff was taken
+  against. That is already true of any JSON Patch — section 5 makes a patch a sequence — but it is
+  worth stating here, because a reader who takes one operation out of the middle of a patch and
+  applies it alone will land somewhere else.
+- The alignment is the one part of the toolkit that is a choice rather than a reading of an RFC. It
+  can be changed — a different tie-break, a different estimate for a changed pair — without changing
+  what any patch means, as long as the tests that assert exact patches are updated with it.
