+
+# createPatch emits add, remove and replace: no move, copy or test
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` implements six operations, and a patch `createPatch` produces is free to use any of
+them. Three of the six are there for cases a diff could plausibly detect:
+
+- `move`, for a value that is at a different location in the second version — the reordering of an
+  array that dl-014 describes as a removal and an addition, or a member renamed in an object;
+- `copy`, for a value that appears in the second version where another value already equal to it
+  sits;
+- `test`, which a patch uses to state what the document must look like before it is applied, and which
+  dl-005 and dl-009 treat as the idiom RFC 6902 offers for a conditional update.
+
+A client sending a change over the network has a reason to want each: `move` and `copy` would make a
+reordering or a duplication shorter, and `test` would make the patch safe to apply to a document that
+has changed in the meantime.
+
+## Decision
+
+`createPatch` emits `add`, `remove` and `replace`, and nothing else.
+
+**`move` and `copy` are not emitted because finding them is a search, not a comparison.** Both ask a
+question about a value that is somewhere *else*: for `move`, is this removed value the same as that
+added one; for `copy`, is this added value already in the document. Answering either means comparing
+values across the whole document rather than at the location the walk has reached, which is a
+different algorithm with its own cost, and the answer is a guess about intent — two records that
+happen to be equal are not evidence that one was moved. What they would buy is a shorter patch, which
+dl-013 has already declined to optimise for: the removal and the addition carry the value once, and
+`move` would carry it not at all, so the saving is real but small next to a diff that is already down
+to what changed. The cases where it is not small — a long array reordered wholesale — are the cases
+where the guess is least reliable.
+
+**`test` is not emitted because the patch states what changed, not what the document must be.** The
+request asks for the change between two versions; a precondition is a different thing, and one whose
+shape only the caller knows. The obvious reading — a `test` for every location the patch touches —
+would roughly double the patch, make every operation's old value travel alongside its new one, and
+turn a patch that is sent once into one that fails for a reason the sender cannot anticipate. A caller
+who wants a conditional update prepends their own `test` to the patch, which is why the patch is
+returned as a fresh array they own; `TestFailedError` is then theirs to catch, as dl-005 intends. The
+README says this where a reader will look for it.
+
+**Nothing in this decision is about what the patch means.** A patch of `add`, `remove` and `replace`
+applies exactly as one that used all six would, by RFC 6902; the other three operations are shorthand,
+and this entry point does not write in shorthand.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A reordered array gives removals and additions, and a renamed member gives a `remove` and an `add`
+  carrying the value. Both are longer than they could be, and neither is wrong.
+- A caller can tell what the patch does by reading it: three operations, each naming one location,
+  with no `from` to follow and no precondition to check. That is worth something to the services that
+  log patches.
+- Detecting a `move` or a `copy` is a compatible addition later: it would shorten patches without
+  changing what they mean, so it belongs behind an option or in a named entry point of its own, and
+  the tests that assert exact patches say which ones it would change.
+- A caller who needs a conditional update has to write the `test` themselves. That is one line and the
+  only place they can decide what the precondition is; the toolkit guessing it would be worse.
