+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch copies both documents and publishes no error class"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# createPatch copies both documents and publishes no error class
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch` is the third entry point to take two JSON documents, and the first to return a *patch*
+rather than a document. Two settled questions have to be answered again for it, and one is new.
+
+dl-004 and dl-010 fixed the immutability contract: an entry point is a pure function of its
+arguments, nothing it returns is entangled with anything it was given, and `cloneJson` is the one
+place where the JSON data model is enforced. What `createPatch` returns is a patch, and the values in
+that patch come from the second document — so the question dl-010 answered for the patch argument of
+`applyMergePatch` comes back with the sides swapped: a naive diff returns operations whose `value`
+members *are* the subtrees of the document handed in.
+
+dl-011 decided that `applyMergePatch` publishes no error class, because RFC 7386 gives a merge patch
+no way to be invalid. Whether the same holds here has to be checked rather than assumed: a diff takes
+two documents, and might have found a pair it could not describe.
+
+The new question is what the data-model check does with a diff's *first* argument, which the patch
+never carries a value from.
+
+## Decision
+
+**Both documents are copied with `cloneJson` before anything is compared, and the patch is built out
+of the copies.** So an operation's `value` is a copy of a copy of nothing the caller holds: changing
+the second document afterwards cannot change a patch already taken, and changing the patch cannot
+change the document. Neither argument is read again after it is copied, so neither can be modified.
+This is dl-004's contract and dl-010's reasoning, applied to the direction a diff runs in.
+
+**The copy of the first document is taken too, and only read.** It is thrown away, like the copy of
+the target that dl-010 takes for a merge patch that replaces it, and for the same reason: `cloneJson`
+is the data-model boundary, and the boundary does not move with what an argument happens to contain.
+`createPatch({ a: undefined }, 1)` raises rather than returning a `replace` of the root — a caller who
+hands in a document the toolkit cannot hold should hear about it whichever side of the comparison it is
+on, and not only when the diff happens to look at the offending value. A check that walked the
+document without copying it would do, and there is no such function in the toolkit: dl-004 made the
+copy the check, and inventing a second way in to save an allocation here would put the boundary in two
+places.
+
+**`cloneJson`'s `TypeError` is re-thrown with the argument named**, as `applyMergePatch` does:
+`the "from" document is not a JSON value: …` or `the "to" document is not a JSON value: …`, with the
+original as `cause`. The names are quoted because `from` and `to` are ordinary English words, and an
+unquoted "the from document" reads as a typo.
+
+**No error class is published, and `createPatch` cannot fail.** Any two JSON values have a difference
+that `add`, `remove` and `replace` can express — the extreme being a `replace` of the root — so there
+is no pair the diff has to refuse, no pointer handed in to parse, and no precondition. The one throw is
+dl-004's `TypeError` for something that is not a JSON document at all, which dl-011 already settled is
+a programming error on the caller's side rather than a failure of the operation. A caller diffs without
+a `try`/`catch`; the patch they get is for `applyPatch` to accept, and `InvalidPatchError` is not
+reachable from a patch this function built.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The three entry points have one contract between them: pure functions of their arguments, nothing
+  shared with what they return, `TypeError` and nothing else for a value outside the JSON data model.
+  A caller guards once, around any of them.
+- A diff costs a copy of both documents on top of the comparison. The copy of the first is pure
+  overhead for the diff itself and pays for the data-model check; as in dl-004 and dl-010, the answer
+  if it ever matters is a separate entry point, not a weaker boundary.
+- `createPatch` has no error of its own to document, so the README states the absence, as it does for
+  `applyMergePatch`: a reader who expects a `createPatch` failure mode by analogy with `applyPatch`
+  will not find one.
+- The patch is a fresh array of plain objects the caller owns. They may extend it — dl-015 expects them
+  to, when they want a `test` — and `applyPatch` copies every value out of it again, so a patch handed
+  straight back is not entangled with the document it produces either.
