+---
+
+# Produce a JSON Patch between two documents createPatch
+
+## Request
+
+Our clients now want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the
+whole new version: add a way to produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902), exported as
+`createPatch(from, to)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns a patch that turns `from` into `to` when
+applied, and must not mutate either input. The patch should describe what changed and nothing more:
+when two versions differ in one small place, sending the whole document again is not acceptable.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test-first, red before green: `test/json-patch-diff.test.ts` was written and run against a missing
+`createPatch` — `SyntaxError: The requested module '../src/index.ts' does not provide an export named
+'createPatch'` — then `src/json-patch-diff.ts` was added until all 111 cases passed. The 239 cases of
+the earlier tasks were kept green throughout.
+
+The request fixes what the patch must *do* — apply to `from` and give `to` — in one sentence, and the
+work is in the sentence after it, which says what the patch may not contain. Both halves are asserted
+in the tests: every case checks that `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` is `to`, and the cases
+that matter also assert the exact patch, operation by operation, because "nothing more" is only
+visible in what the patch does not say.
+
+### Code
+
+- `src/json-patch-diff.ts` — `createPatch`, the recursive comparison of two documents, the member-by-
+  member comparison of two objects, the element-by-element alignment of two arrays with the walk that
+  turns it into operations, and the copy that names which of the two documents was not a JSON value.
+- `src/index.ts` — publishes `createPatch`, next to `applyPatch` whose patches it produces.
+- `src/json-value.ts`, `src/json-patch.ts` — `jsonEquals`, the equality of RFC 6902, section 4.6, moved
+  out of `json-patch.ts`, where only `test` used it, into the data-model module as a shared internal
+  (dl-007). The diff asks that question of every pair of values it considers, and one reading of the
+  section in the library is the point of that decision. Committed on its own, before the feature.
+
+`createPatch` copies both documents, then walks them together: values equal as JSON produce nothing,
+two objects are compared member by member in sorted order, two arrays are aligned as an edit script
+over their elements, and anything else is a `replace` of the location — at the root included. The
+patch holds `add`, `remove` and `replace` only, in the order they must be applied, and its array
+indices are those of the document as the patch is applied, which the operations themselves shift.
+
+### Tests
+
+`test/json-patch-diff.test.ts`, 111 cases: documents equal as JSON, including the same document twice
+and members written in a different order, giving an empty patch; thirteen pairs of roots whose kind or
+value changes, each one `replace` of `""`; the members of an object added, removed, replaced, set to
+`null` — which a merge patch cannot do — told apart from absent, descended into rather than replaced,
+created whole, named in a fixed order, escaped as JSON Pointer escapes them, named with the empty
+string, with `-`, with `__proto__` and with `toString`; the elements of an array appended at `-`,
+inserted at the front and in the middle, removed from either end and from the middle, replaced,
+descended into, shifted without being rewritten, and moved without being resent, with duplicates,
+nested arrays, an array emptied and an empty array filled; the patch carrying nothing the two
+documents share, using no operation but the three, being JSON itself and being the same patch every
+time; both documents left alone and sharing nothing with the patch in either direction; nine kinds of
+value outside the JSON data model refused in either document, with the message naming which one and
+keeping the cause, a cycle refused as well, and the refusal standing even when the other document
+would have settled the comparison on its own; and
+a table of thirty-one documents of every shape, each turned into each of the others — 961 patches, all
+of which `applyPatch` turns the one into the other with.
+
+### Documentation
+
+`README.md` gained a "JSON Patch diff" building block, between "JSON Patch" and "JSON Merge Patch":
+the round-trip property first, then what the patch leaves out and why, how arrays are compared and
+what that means for the indices, the three operations it uses and the three it does not, the shortest-
+patch trade it declines, the immutability contract, and the cost of comparing two long arrays. Every
+example in it and in the module's doc comment was run as written.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- dl-013 — the patch names only the locations the two versions disagree about and carries no value they
+  share; fewest operations is rejected as the objective, because it licenses resending a parent whose
+  members have not changed. Members are visited in sorted order so the patch is a function of the two
+  documents alone.
+- dl-014 — two arrays are aligned as an edit script over their elements: the elements they share are
+  kept wherever they moved to, the common ends are matched before the table, an aligned pair is
+  compared rather than replaced, ties are broken in a fixed order, indices are counted as the patch is
+  applied, and an append is written at `-`.
+- dl-015 — `add`, `remove` and `replace` only: `move` and `copy` would need a search across the
+  document and a guess about intent, and `test` states a precondition that is the caller's to write.
+- dl-016 — both documents are copied up front, the patch is built out of the copies, the copy of the
+  first is taken for the data-model check and thrown away, the `TypeError` names which document was not
+  JSON, and no error class is published because any two JSON values have a difference.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 350 tests, 350 pass, 0 fail (111 for the diff, 239 for the three earlier blocks).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, `createPatch` included.
+- `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json --noUnusedLocals --noUnusedParameters` reports nothing.
+- The README's five examples and the module's doc-comment example were run as written and produce the
+  documented results.
+- Two sanity runs outside the test suite, on documents larger than a unit test: one element changed in
+  the middle of a 5 000-element array of objects gives one `replace` naming that element's member, and
+  one element inserted at the front of the same array gives one `add` — in tens of milliseconds, most
+  of it the copy of the two documents.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- The follow-ups of the earlier tasks stand: no linter, `package.json` still declares no `main` or
+  `exports`, and `dist/*.d.ts` keeps the `.ts` import extensions that `dist/*.js` has rewritten.
+- Two long arrays that differ in the middle cost a table of one cell per pair of elements (dl-014). No
+  caller has arrays of that size today; if one appears, the thing to add is an entry point with a
+  different trade, not a cheaper alignment that would quietly resend data.
+- `move` and `copy` detection, and an option to emit `test` operations, are both compatible additions
+  that dl-015 leaves open. Each needs a caller asking for it before it is worth the search it costs.
+- The toolkit can now produce a JSON Patch between two documents but not a JSON Merge Patch. The
+  converse of task-004 — a merge patch between two documents, which is a much simpler walk with the
+  format's limits (no explicit `null`, arrays replaced whole) — is the obvious next building block if
+  clients that send merge patches want the same thing.
